Add Rust-side scope resolution pass

Introduce a `scopes` module that walks the AST and builds a graph of
`Scope`s with `parent` pointers and per-scope symbol tables. The pass
uses two scoping disciplines, with corresponding `UnorderedNodeId` and
`OrderedNodeId` wrapper enums and separate `flat` / `entry` / `exit`
side tables:

* Unordered (modules, model bodies, decls): one scope per node,
  declaration order does not affect visibility.
* Ordered (rule bodies, arg-list descendants, expressions inside
  them): entry/exit scopes per node, sibling nodes chain so bindings
  introduced earlier are visible in later siblings.

Every `VarTerm` occurrence and every named `ArgDecl` extends the
current scope with a fresh child scope containing the corresponding
`Symbol`, so later occurrences shadow earlier ones and the binder of a
variable is recoverable by walking the parent chain until the name is
no longer in scope.

Emit `SymbolDeclaredTwice` errors from this pass for duplicates in
unordered scopes; the check is order-sensitive (first vs. second
declaration) so it belongs on the Rust side.

The pass currently runs only to surface these errors; ambient-name
resolution and the resolution-dependent checks (undeclared symbol,
bad symbol kind, arity) continue to come from eqlog-eqlog and will
move in later commits.
